## The Nimbus Access Card

The **Access Card** from Nimbus shows your access needs using simple symbols, such as needing a **free companion ticket (+1)**, step-free access or captions. It is accepted at many **theatres, concert venues, stadiums and attractions** across the UK.

- it costs **£15** for **3 years** at the time of writing
- apply online and upload proof, such as a benefit award letter or a letter from a health professional

## Hynt in Wales

**Hynt** is a free scheme for theatres and arts centres in **Wales**. Cardholders get a **free ticket for a carer or personal assistant** at participating venues. Apply on the Hynt website.

## Heritage sites

- **National Trust:** free entry for an **essential companion**. You can apply for an Essential Companion card to show at the door.
- **English Heritage:** free entry for **one companion or carer** with a disabled visitor.
- **Cadw** (Wales): free entry for **carers** accompanying a disabled visitor.
- **Museums and galleries:** most national museums are free, and many offer relaxed openings and sensory maps.

## Theme parks, zoos and attractions

Most large attractions offer **free or discounted carer tickets**, and some have **ride access passes** so you do not have to queue in the main line. Each has its own rules and proof it accepts, so check their accessibility page before you go.

## Sport and live music

Most football clubs and big venues have **access ticketing** with free or cheaper companion tickets. Contact the club or venue's **disability liaison officer** or access booking line.

## The Max Card

The **Max Card** is a discount card for **families of children with additional needs**, given out by many local councils and charities. It gives free or discounted entry at hundreds of attractions. Ask your council or local parent carer forum.
